<style>
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    min-height: clamp(600px, calc(100svh - 140px), 820px);
    background: var(--sb-ink);
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* --- Image stage ------------------------------------------------------- */

  .hero__stage {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    z-index: 0;
  }

  .hero__slide {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    opacity: 0;
    /* Audit 1.3 — opacity only. Nothing translates, nothing scales. */
    transition: opacity var(--sb-dur-fade) ease-in-out;
  }

  .hero__slide[data-active] { opacity: 1; }

  /* Sits exactly over its own poster <Photo>, and is invisible until the script
     has it playing — so a film that never loads leaves the still in place with
     nothing to notice. */
  .hero__video {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity var(--sb-dur-fade) ease-in-out;
  }

  .hero__video[data-playing] { opacity: 1; }

  /* Square. <Photo> carries a 16px radius for the card contexts it usually sits
     in; a full-bleed banner is not one of those, and the rounded corners showed
     against the stage. */
  .hero__slide :global(.photo) { border-radius: 0; }

  /* Warm the photography toward the brand without tinting it into a filter.
     Kept light: a heavy maroon wash makes the whole frame read as sepia, and
     this school needs to look current, not archival. */
  /* ⚠ HALVED, 0.12 → 0.06. A maroon wash across the whole frame is orange over
     every pixel of the photograph, which is the other half of "the orange
     colour is becoming dominant". At 0.06 it still takes the blue edge off a
     hard midday sky without reading as a filter. */
  .hero__tint {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: rgba(61, 0, 0, 0.06);
  }

  /* Two-axis scrim.
     The stock bottom-weighted scrim assumes a photograph that darkens toward
     the base. The real campus shot is bright at BOTH ends — sky above, sunlit
     grass below — and it left the gold eyebrow effectively invisible and the
     headline marginal.
     So the vertical scrim is joined by a left-weighted one, which lands exactly
     where the copy sits (columns 1–7) and clears by the two-thirds mark, so the
     buildings on the right stay fully legible. */
  /* ⚠ LIGHTENED, AND THE WEIGHT MOVED OFF THE MIDDLE OF THE FRAME. The client's
     note was "the dark overlay is hiding the building at the back". The two
     ramps multiply, so the old pair compounded to ~0.66 at the centre of the
     image and ~0.30 over the buildings on the right — the campus was being
     dimmed everywhere, not just under the words.
     Now the horizontal ramp keeps its bite only where the copy actually is and
     clears by the 72% mark, and the vertical one is roughly halved. Measured on
     the composite: ~0.93 bottom-left (where the headline sits), ~0.42 at centre,
     ~0.18 over the right-hand buildings. The copy keeps its ground; the campus
     comes back.
     ⚠ READABILITY IS PAID FOR IN TYPE, NOT IN MORE BLACK — the headline, deck
     and motto carry their own text-shadow below. If a future photograph needs
     more contrast, reach for that first.

     ⚠⚠ LIGHTENED AGAIN, AND THIS IS NEAR THE FLOOR. The client asked for less
     overlay a second time, so the left edge came down from 0.86 to 0.72 and the
     vertical ramp from 0.52 to 0.44.

     ⚠ THE DECK IS THE BINDING CONSTRAINT, NOT THE HEADLINE. Measured by hiding
     the copy, sampling the exact rectangle each block occupies and taking the
     BRIGHTEST background pixel under it: the deck is small text and needs
     4.5:1, and its 46ch measure reaches halfway across the frame, where the
     ramp has already fallen away. These values give 5.05:1. The previous
     settings gave 6.25:1, and dropping the left edge to 0.70 gives 4.37:1 —
     under the line. So there is roughly half a stop left, not a free hand.

     ⚠ STEEPENING THE FALLOFF INSTEAD DOES NOT WORK, and was tried: pulling the
     stops in to clear the buildings sooner moved the building region's mean
     luminance only 0.231 → 0.279 while the deck fell to 2.85:1. The buildings
     on the right are already almost unveiled; what reads as "heavy" is the left
     ramp, which is the half doing the work. */
  /* ⚠⚠ THE FIRST LAYER IS A FEATHERED POOL BEHIND THE COPY, AND IT IS THE
     ANSWER TO A TRADE-OFF, NOT A DECORATION. Read this before touching it.

     When the copy moved down off the building (client note, 17 Sep 2026), it
     landed on the bright driveway and legibility collapsed — measured at the
     real ink bounds, the headline fell to 2.40:1 against a 3.0 threshold and
     the motto to 2.17:1 against 4.5.

     The obvious fix — deepen the two linear ramps — was measured and REJECTED.
     Getting the headline to pass that way cost 35% of the building's
     brightness (mean luminance 0.216 → 0.142), which breaks the other half of
     the same client instruction: the building must stay clearly visible.

     This radial darkens only where the words are. It is an ellipse centred on
     the copy block and faded to zero well before the facade, so:
         headline  2.40 → 4.73:1   (threshold 3.0)
         motto     2.17 → 5.61:1   (threshold 4.5)
         building  0.216 → 0.193   — an 11% loss, against 35% for the ramps.

     ⚠ IT MUST STAY FULLY FEATHERED. Tighten the stops and it stops being a
     gradient and starts being a visible plate behind the text — which is the
     exact "solid/text box background" the client asked twice to remove.
     ⚠ IF THE COPY MOVES, MOVE THIS WITH IT. The ellipse is positioned on the
     copy block, not on the frame. */
  .hero__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background:
      radial-gradient(
        ellipse 72% 52% at 32% 72%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.82) 0%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.66) 42%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.28) 72%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0) 100%
      ),
      linear-gradient(
        to right,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.72) 0%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.6) 26%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.32) 46%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.08) 62%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0) 72%
      ),
      linear-gradient(
        to top,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.44) 0%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.19) 32%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.05) 62%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.1) 100%
      );
  }

  /* On narrow screens the copy spans the full width, so the horizontal ramp
     would leave the right-hand words sitting on bare photograph. */
  /* From tablet down the copy spans most of the width, so the horizontal ramp
     no longer covers it and the vertical one has to do all the work. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    /* The copy block reaches roughly 70% up the frame here, so the ramp has to
       hold under it — but the old curve sat at 0.8 all the way to the 78% mark
       and blacked the tablet hero out almost completely, which is the same
       complaint the desktop scrim just answered. It now releases earlier and
       ends much lighter, so the top third of the photograph reads; the type
       below carries its own shadow for the rest. */
    .hero__scrim {
      background:
        linear-gradient(
          to top,
          rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.86) 0%,
          rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.78) 50%,
          rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.54) 72%,
          rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.2) 88%,
          rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.1) 100%
        );
    }
  }

  /* --- Content ----------------------------------------------------------- */

  .hero__content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--sb-cols), minmax(0, 1fr));
    column-gap: var(--sb-gutter);
    align-items: end;
    /* ⚠ ASYMMETRIC, AND THE BOTTOM VALUE IS LOAD-BEARING. The grid is
       `align-items: end`, so the copy sits one bottom-padding up from the
       hero's floor — which means this number decides how far the headline
       reaches into the photograph. It was 96px top and bottom, which lifted the
       stack onto the building's facade. 40px at the bottom drops it into the
       ground band; raising it again puts the words back on the building. */
    padding-block: var(--sb-11) var(--sb-8);
  }

  /* Asymmetric: columns 1–7 of 12. Never centred. */
  .hero__copy {
    /* ⚠ TEN COLUMNS, NOT EIGHT. .hero__meta used to hold 9–12 and is now
       display:none, so the copy is free to take the room — and it needs it: the
       headline runs to three lines and the stat card is a three-up row beside
       an icon disc each. Columns 11–12 stay clear so the card never reaches the
       right margin, which is where the reference has it end. */
    grid-column: 1 / span 10;
  }

  /* .hero__badge and .hero__badge-dot were deleted with the markup they styled
     — see the note beside the eyebrow. Dead rules for an element no page emits
     are how a stylesheet stops being readable. */

  /* ⚠⚠ THE WHITE PLATE IS GONE, AND IT WAS THE ONE THING ON THIS HERO THAT
     ACTUALLY WAS A BOX. It was rgba(255,255,255,0.88) + blur(14px) — an
     88%-opaque panel 623×102px sitting on the photograph, which is precisely
     the "solid/text box background" the client asked twice to remove. The
     figures now sit on the picture like every other line of hero copy: no
     ground, no border, no blur, no shadow — just type with the same shadow the
     headline carries, separated by hairlines.
     ⚠ DO NOT REINTRODUCE A PLATE HERE. If these become hard to read, the answer
     is the text-shadow below, not a panel. */
  .hero__stats {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--sb-6);
    margin-top: var(--sb-5);
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
    box-shadow: none;
    backdrop-filter: none;
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: none;
  }

  .hero__stat {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--sb-4);
  }

  .hero__stat + .hero__stat {
    padding-left: var(--sb-6);
    /* Was ink at 12% — invisible now the plate is gone. */
    border-left: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.28);
  }

  .hero__stat-body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1px;
  }

  /* The orange disc. This is where the reference spends its orange inside the
     card — the figures themselves stay ink, which is what keeps three big
     numerals from turning the plate into another orange block. */
  /* ⚠ THE DISC IS GONE WITH THE PLATE IT BELONGED TO. A pale-wash circle reads
     as a design element on a white card and as a smudge on a photograph — and
     three of them at 44px were also 44px of the height this stack had to lose.
     The glyph stays, at label size, in the same white as the figure. */
  .hero__stat-icon {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
    color: var(--sb-on-dark-78);
  }

  .hero__stat-icon svg { width: 20px; height: 20px; display: block;
    filter: drop-shadow(0 1px 6px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.55)); }

  .hero__stat-value {
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 26px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    /* ⚠ WHITE, NOT INK — the plate that made ink legible is gone. */
    color: #fff;
    text-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.45), 0 4px 18px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.5);
  }

  .hero__stat-label {
    font-size: var(--sb-caption);
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--sb-on-dark-78);
    text-shadow: 0 1px 8px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.55);
  }

  /* The motto is set in white, not gold.
     Measured over the real campus photograph, gold ran at 2.89:1 average and
     1.31:1 at worst — and the fix is not a heavier scrim: gold (#C9A227, L=0.38)
     needs a ground darker than L=0.046 to clear 4.5:1, which would mean blacking
     out the photograph entirely. Gold stays as the rule above, where it is
     decoration rather than text. */
  /* The motto, now UNDER the deck rather than over the headline. Understated on
     purpose — it is a tagline, not a second heading, so it stays at label size
     and at 78% white rather than competing with the <h1> above it. */
  /* ⚠ NO BACKGROUND, NO PANEL — the client's words were "transparent
     background with no solid/text box background". There never was a box here
     (the darkening is a gradient across the whole stage, not a plate behind the
     type) and none is added now. The text-shadow is what carries it. */
  .hero__motto {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0 0 var(--sb-8);
    overflow: hidden;
    /* The measure the motto scrolls within. Held short on purpose: a strip
       running the full column width would sweep across the building, which is
       the thing this change exists to stop. */
    /* 38ch, up from 30ch: the fades below eat a third of the strip at each
       end, and at 30ch that left too little clear centre — the eye met a
       half-dissolved word before it met a whole one. Still well short of the
       column width, so the strip never sweeps across the building. */
    max-width: 38ch;
    color: var(--sb-on-dark-78);
    text-shadow: 0 1px 8px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.55);
    /* Fades both ends so the line enters and leaves rather than being clipped
       against a hard edge — a cut-off letter reads as a layout fault. */
    /* ⚠ THE FADE ZONES ARE WIDE ON PURPOSE. At 12% the partial word entering
       each end ("…CIPLINE", "DU…") was still legible enough to read as broken
       text rather than as motion. 24% dissolves the fragment before the eye
       resolves it, which is the whole job of the mask. */
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, transparent 0%, rgba(0,0,0,0.35) 18%, #000 34%, #000 66%, rgba(0,0,0,0.35) 82%, transparent 100%);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, transparent 0%, rgba(0,0,0,0.35) 18%, #000 34%, #000 66%, rgba(0,0,0,0.35) 82%, transparent 100%);
  }

  .hero__motto-track {
    display: flex;
    width: max-content;
    gap: var(--sb-7);
    /* ⚠ -50%, AND THE COPIES ARE WHY. The track holds the motto four times; at
       exactly half its width the second pair sits where the first pair started,
       so the loop is seamless with no measuring in JavaScript. Change the copy
       count and this fraction changes with it. */
    animation: hero-motto 18s linear infinite;
  }

  .hero__motto:hover .hero__motto-track,
  .hero__motto:focus-within .hero__motto-track { animation-play-state: paused; }

  @keyframes hero-motto {
    from { transform: translateX(0); }
    to   { transform: translateX(-50%); }
  }

  /* ⚠ REDUCED MOTION STOPS IT DEAD and drops the extra copies, so the motto is
     a plain static line exactly as it was before this change. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .hero__motto {
      max-width: none;
      -webkit-mask-image: none;
      mask-image: none;
    }
    .hero__motto-track { animation: none; }
    .hero__motto-track span[aria-hidden='true'] { display: none; }
  }

  /* The eyebrow returns, with the reference's short orange rule after it. The
     rule is a flex child rather than a ::after so it takes the leftover space
     up to a cap — a fixed-width pseudo-element sat at a different distance from
     the text at every viewport. */
  .hero__eyebrow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--sb-4);
    color: #fff;
    margin: 0 0 var(--sb-5);
    text-shadow: 0 1px 8px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.5);
  }

  .hero__eyebrow-rule {
    flex: 0 1 56px;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--sb-orange-tint);
  }

  .hero__title {
    color: #fff;
    /* Buys back the contrast the lightened scrim gives up, without darkening a
       single pixel of the photograph. Two stops: a tight one for edge
       definition against a bright wall, a wide soft one for the general ground. */
    text-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.45),
      0 4px 22px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.5);
    /* FLUID, so the chosen break holds at every width rather than only at the
       two where it happened to fit.
       Line 1 is a constant 10.26em ("Where Ballia's children" at this tracking),
       and the eight-column measure is about 0.58vw. 5.2vw keeps 10.26em inside
       that at every size in the range — a fixed 76px did not: it needed 780px
       against 762px at 1240, and broke into three lines there while looking
       correct at 1200.
       No max-width: a measure's only job here would be to stop line 1 fitting,
       and `text-wrap: balance` is gone because balancing fights a break we
       have already chosen. */
    /* ⚠ 3.9vw, DOWN FROM 5.2vw, AND IT IS A CLEARANCE FIGURE NOT A TASTE ONE.
       Line 1 is now "Where Ballia's Future Leaders" — 29 characters, which at
       5.2vw would need ~1.1× the ten-column measure and wrap straight back to
       three lines. 3.9vw keeps it on one line from 1024px up, and keeps the
       whole copy stack inside the empty ground band beneath the building. */
    font-size: clamp(30px, 3.9vw, 56px);
    max-width: none;
    margin-bottom: var(--sb-5);
  }

  /* ⚠ --sb-orange-tint, NOT --sb-violet. The primary orange (#c93c0a) is a
     light-ground colour; over a photograph it goes muddy and loses the contrast
     that makes this line the accent it is meant to be. */
  .hero__accent {
    color: var(--sb-orange-tint);
  }

  /* ⚠ 0.58em — THE NUMBER THAT CARRIES THE HIERARCHY. "Take Shape" reads as a
     smaller third line rather than a continuation of the headline, which was
     the client's note two rounds ago and is still true of the reference. It is
     white here, because the reference spends the headline's orange on
     "Leaders"; it also drops to weight 600, so it settles rather than shouts. */
  /* Each line is its own block box, so each carries its own leading — see the
     note beside the markup. */
  .hero__line { display: block; }

  .hero__take {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.58em;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    /* 1.1 of 0.58em, not 1.08 of 1em: the leading that lands this line the same
       optical distance under "Leaders" as "Leaders" sits under line 1. */
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin-top: 0.04em;
  }

  .hero__deck {
    color: var(--sb-on-dark-90);
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin-bottom: var(--sb-5);
    text-shadow: 0 1px 10px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.5);
  }

  .hero__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--sb-4);
  }

  .hero__cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--sb-3);
  }

  .hero__cta-icon { width: 18px; height: 18px; flex: 0 0 auto; }
  .hero__cta-play { width: 22px; height: 22px; flex: 0 0 auto; }

  /* --- Meta: editorial caption + controls, bottom right ------------------ */

  /* See the note beside the markup: hidden, not deleted. */
  .hero__meta { display: none; }

  .hero__caption {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-end;
    gap: var(--sb-2);
    padding-right: var(--sb-5);
    border-right: 2px solid var(--sb-gold);
    transition: opacity var(--sb-dur-fade) ease-in-out;
  }

  .hero__caption.is-fading { opacity: 0; }

  .hero__caption-tag { color: var(--sb-gold); }

  .hero__caption-text {
    font-size: var(--sb-caption);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--sb-on-dark-78);
    max-width: 34ch;
  }


  /* --- Tablet ------------------------------------------------------------- */

  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    .hero__copy { grid-column: 1 / span 10; }
    .hero__title { max-width: none; }
  }

  /* Below ~900 the caption competes with the deck for the same optical line. */
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .hero__caption-text { display: none; }
    .hero__caption { border-right: 0; padding-right: 0; }
    .hero__caption-tag { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5); }
  }

  /* --- Mobile: 4:5 crop, stacked actions, controls below copy ------------- */

  /* ⚠ THE BREAK IS NO LONGER DROPPED ON NARROW SCREENS, and that rule is gone
     rather than tightened. It existed because "Take Shape" used to be a plate
     set at the full headline size: below ~480 the first phrase could not fit on
     one line anyway, so the manual break was dropped and the whole thing wrapped
     as one heading. "Take Shape" is now a smaller second line, so the break IS
     the hierarchy — without it the phrase ran inline after "Leaders" at 390,
     which is exactly what the client asked not to happen. The first phrase wraps
     naturally over two lines at that width; it no longer needs the break. */

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    /* ⚠⚠ ON MOBILE THE FILM IS A BLOCK AT THE TOP, NOT A BACKGROUND.
       This is the difference between this block and the desktop layout above,
       and it is the whole point of it.

       DESKTOP: .hero__stage is position:absolute inset:0, so the film sits
       BEHIND everything and the copy is laid over it through a scrim.

       MOBILE: the stage is taken out of that and put back into normal flow as a
       fixed-height band, and the copy sits BELOW it on the hero's own solid
       --sb-ink. Nothing is layered over the film and nothing of the film runs
       behind the headline.

       An earlier pass did this the other way — full-bleed film with a gradient
       over the lower two-thirds. It technically showed the campus, but the
       building stayed visible behind the headline and the client's note was
       exactly that: the video should be at the top with the content below it,
       not playing underneath the text.

       ⚠ --hero-band IS THE ONLY DIMENSION TO TOUCH. The stage height, the fade
       into the solid ground and the play button all derive from it. */
    .hero {
      --hero-band: clamp(190px, 30svh, 250px);
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
      /* Height comes from band + copy now, not from a viewport clamp — a fixed
         min-height would leave dead ground under the stat card on tall phones. */
      min-height: 0;
      background: var(--sb-ink);
    }

    .hero__stage {
      position: relative;
      inset: auto;
      flex: 0 0 auto;
      width: 100%;
      height: var(--hero-band);
    }

    /* Only a soft landing at the bottom edge of the film, so it meets the solid
       ground without a hard seam. The film itself is left completely unwashed —
       covering it is what the previous version got wrong. */
    .hero__scrim {
      background: linear-gradient(
        to bottom,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0) 0,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0) 58%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.5) 86%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 1) 100%
      );
    }

    /* The maroon wash exists to sit a scrim on a photograph. There is no scrim
       over the film here, so it would just dirty it. */
    .hero__tint { background: none; }

    .hero__content {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
      padding-block: var(--sb-7) var(--sb-8);
      gap: var(--sb-6);
    }


    /* ⚠ SAME HIERARCHY ON A PHONE, which is the client's point 9. The headline
       caps at 46px here, so "Take Shape" holding 0.42em of that would fall to
       19px and read as body copy — it takes a floor of its own instead, still
       comfortably under the line above it. */
    .hero__take {
      font-size: clamp(19px, 5.2vw, 25px);
      /* Measured, not guessed: at 0.28em the band under "Leaders" ran 18px
         against 12px under line 1 — the same unevenness the desktop rule just
         fixed, reintroduced by this override. */
      margin-top: 0.1em;
    }

    /* ⚠ ON A PHONE THE MOTTO IS STATIC, NOT A MARQUEE, AND THAT IS THE RIGHT
       CALL RATHER THAN A COMPROMISE. The strip is ~323px inside a ~350px
       column here, and the fades that stop a half-word reading as broken text
       eat ~110px at each end — which leaves a clear window too narrow to ever
       show "DUTY · DEVOTION · DISCIPLINE" whole. The result was a fragment at
       both edges and no complete motto at any moment.
       The line fits the column at rest, so it simply sits there: no track, no
       mask, no duplicate copies. The client's brief said a marquee "if
       suitable" — at this width it is not. Desktop keeps it. */
    .hero__motto {
      margin-bottom: var(--sb-6);
      max-width: none;
      -webkit-mask-image: none;
      mask-image: none;
    }

    .hero__motto-track { animation: none; }
    .hero__motto-track span[aria-hidden='true'] { display: none; }

    /* ⚠ THE TITLE IS DELIBERATELY SMALLER THAN THE FLUID DESKTOP RULE. At 5.2vw
       the headline ran to three very deep lines on a phone and pushed the stat
       card off the fold entirely — the reference has the figures visible. This
       caps it without touching the desktop curve. */
    .hero__title {
      max-width: none;
      margin-bottom: var(--sb-4);
      font-size: clamp(34px, 9.4vw, 46px);
      line-height: 1.04;
    }

    .hero__deck {
      max-width: none;
      /* The motto now sits between the deck and the buttons and carries the
         gap; the deck only needs to clear it. */
      margin-bottom: var(--sb-4);
      font-size: clamp(15px, 4.2vw, 17px);
    }

    /* The card keeps the light plate it has on desktop, but stacks the icon
       above the figure again: three icon-beside-figure rows will not fit across
       a 320px phone without the labels crushing. */
    /* ⚠ THE PLATE IS GONE HERE TOO. This override carried its own copy of the
       white card (0.94 alpha + blur) and survived the desktop rule being
       stripped — a second definition of the same box, in a media query, is
       exactly how "we removed the box" ships with the box still on phones.
       If you remove a treatment above, grep the file for the class before
       calling it done. */
    .hero__stats {
      display: flex;
      width: 100%;
      gap: 0;
      padding: 0;
      margin-top: var(--sb-5);
      background: none;
      border: 0;
      border-radius: 0;
      backdrop-filter: none;
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: none;
    }

    /* Equal thirds, so the three-word label under #1 cannot widen its column
       and squeeze the other two. */
    .hero__stat {
      flex: 1 1 0;
      min-width: 0;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 4px;
      text-align: center;
      padding-inline: 6px;
    }

    .hero__stat + .hero__stat {
      padding-left: 6px;
      /* Ink at 12% was for the white plate; it is invisible on the photograph. */
      border-left: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.28);
    }

    .hero__stat-body {
      align-items: center;
      text-align: center;
    }

    .hero__stat-icon {
      width: 34px;
      height: 34px;
      margin-bottom: 2px;
    }

    .hero__stat-icon svg { width: 18px; height: 18px; }
    .hero__stat-value { font-size: clamp(19px, 5.4vw, 24px); }
    .hero__stat-label {
      font-size: clamp(9.5px, 2.7vw, 11px);
      line-height: 1.25;
      text-wrap: balance;
    }


    .hero__actions { flex-direction: column; }
    .hero__actions .btn { width: 100%; }

    .hero__eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--sb-4); }
  }

  /* Below ~400 the eyebrow wraps to two lines and the rule ends up alone on the
     second, reading as a stray dash rather than as part of the line. */
  @media (max-width: 400px) {
    .hero__eyebrow-rule { display: none; }

  }
  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     DESKTOP HERO — VIDEO ABOVE, FLOATING EDITORIAL PANEL BELOW
     Client spec, 17 September 2026.

     ⚠⚠ EVERY RULE IN THIS BLOCK IS INSIDE min-width:1024px, AND THAT IS THE
     WHOLE CONTRACT. The client's words were "all these changes are for only
     desktop devices, on mobile don't change anything". Mobile is driven by the
     max-width blocks above, which are untouched; nothing below this line can
     reach a phone or a tablet. Do not move any of it out to a base rule.

     ⚠⚠ THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES, AND HOW NOT TO UNDO IT. The client's complaint
     was that the copy covered the school building. The answer here is NOT a
     darker video and NOT a card laid on top of the building — both were tried
     and both are rejected by name in the spec. The copy is OUT of the video
     entirely: the stage is its own box, and the panel is a sibling that
     overlaps only the stage's bottom edge. The building is therefore
     unobstructed whatever frame the film is on, which is also why the scrim and
     the tint are switched off here — with no text over the picture there is
     nothing left for them to make legible.

     ⚠ THE PANEL IS LAID OUT WITH GRID PLACEMENT, NOT WITH NEW MARKUP. The two
     columns are produced by placing the existing children of .hero__copy on a
     three-track grid. That is deliberate: restructuring the DOM would have
     changed the mobile source order too, and the one thing this change must not
     do is touch mobile.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  /* Neither the in-video UI nor the panel decoration exists below 1024px —
     not hidden, absent, so they stay out of the mobile accessibility tree. */
  .hero__vui,
  .hero__deco,
  .hero__cta-people { display: none; }

  /* ⚠ THE DESKTOP-ONLY WRAPPER, NEUTRALISED EVERYWHERE ELSE. See the note
     beside the markup — `contents` means mobile's box tree is identical to
     what it was before the wrapper existed. */
  .hero__lead { display: contents; }

  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    /* --- 1 · The hero stops being one overlaid box ------------------------ */
    .hero {
      display: block;
      align-items: initial;
      min-height: 0;
      /* ⚠⚠ --sb-sand, AND IT MUST MATCH THE SECTION BELOW EXACTLY. The panel
         overhangs the video by 70px, so the strip either side of that overhang
         is hero background — and it runs straight into ProofStrip, the "50
         Years of Sunbeam" band, which is --sb-sand (#f4ece7). It was --sb-ivory
         (#fbf7f4) here: close enough to look deliberate and wrong enough to
         draw a faint horizontal seam across the page under the card.
         ⚠ IF ProofStrip's GROUND EVER CHANGES, CHANGE THIS WITH IT. The two are
         one continuous surface to a reader; there is no divider between them. */
      background: var(--sb-sand);
      /* The panel's shadow must not be clipped by the section. */
      isolation: auto;
      overflow: visible;
    }

    /* --- 2 · The video, now a block of its own (spec §2: 650–700px) ------- */
    .hero__stage {
      position: relative;
      inset: auto;
      width: 100%;
      height: clamp(650px, 68vh, 700px);
      z-index: 0;
    }

    /* ⚠ OFF, NOT SOFTENED. Spec §2: "no overlay", and explicitly not
       rgba(0,0,0,0.6)/0.7. There is no text on the picture any more, so the
       only thing a scrim could do here is dim the building the client asked us
       to show. The in-video UI carries its own local shading instead. */
    .hero__tint,
    .hero__scrim { display: none; }

    /* --- 3 · In-video UI: motto + ticks left, pause right ----------------- */
    .hero__vui {
      position: absolute;
      inset: auto 0 0 0;
      z-index: 2;
      display: flex;
      align-items: flex-end;
      justify-content: space-between;
      gap: var(--sb-6);
      /* Room above the panel's overhang so nothing here is covered by it.
         ⚠ THE TOP PADDING IS TALL ON PURPOSE — it is the fade's runway. The
         band is bottom-anchored, so padding-top only moves its TOP edge up; the
         controls stay where they are. A short band forced the gradient to go
         from plateau to nothing in ~28px, which reads as a hard-edged strip
         across the picture. */
      padding: 90px clamp(28px, 4vw, 64px) calc(70px + var(--sb-6));
      /* ⚠⚠ A FOOT FOR THE VIDEO CONTROLS — SANCTIONED BY THE SPEC, AND SHAPED
         BY MEASUREMENT. Spec §2 allows "an extremely subtle bottom gradient
         only if required for video controls"; it is required, and here is the
         evidence. Measured against the real footage, white UI over the bright
         driveway ran at 1.38:1 (motto), 1.06:1 (ticks) and 1.19:1 (pause)
         against a 4.5 threshold — unreadable, not merely tight.

         ⚠ THE STOPS TRACK THE CONTROLS, NOT THE FRAME. Measured as % up from
         this band's bottom edge: the panel hides 0–27%, the ticks and the pause
         button sit at 37–54%, and the motto at 59–65%. The plateau therefore
         spans 30–68% and everything above it is runway.

         ⚠ IT DOES NOT REACH THE BUILDING, AND THAT IS THE POINT. The facade's
         mean luminance is identical with this on and off (0.377 either way) —
         if you deepen or extend it, re-measure that number before shipping. */
      background: linear-gradient(to top,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.55) 0%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.80) 30%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.80) 68%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.55) 80%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.18) 92%,
        rgba(18, 16, 15, 0) 100%);
      pointer-events: none;
    }

    .hero__vui-l { display: grid; gap: var(--sb-4); pointer-events: none; }

    .hero__vmotto {
      margin: 0;
      color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
      letter-spacing: 0.18em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      text-shadow: 0 1px 10px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.75);
    }

    .hero__ticks {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 10px;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      list-style: none;
      pointer-events: auto;
    }

    .hero__tick {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 8px;
      /* 44px target (spec §4) without a 44px-tall visual. */
      padding: 14px 0;
      background: none;
      border: 0;
      cursor: pointer;
      color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.62);
      transition: color 220ms ease;
    }

    .hero__tick-n {
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-shadow: 0 1px 8px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.7);
    }

    .hero__tick-rule {
      display: block;
      width: 26px;
      height: 2px;
      border-radius: 2px;
      background: currentColor;
      opacity: 0.55;
      transition: width 260ms ease, opacity 220ms ease;
    }

    .hero__tick:hover,
    .hero__tick:focus-visible { color: #fff; }

    .hero__tick[aria-current="true"] { color: var(--sb-orange-tint); }
    .hero__tick[aria-current="true"] .hero__tick-rule { width: 40px; opacity: 1; }

    .hero__tick:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #fff; outline-offset: 3px; border-radius: 4px; }

    /* Pause — circular glyph, white hairline, no fill (spec §4). */
    .hero__pause {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 10px;
      min-height: 44px;
      padding: 8px 16px 8px 8px;
      background: none;
      border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55);
      border-radius: 999px;
      color: #fff;
      cursor: pointer;
      pointer-events: auto;
      text-shadow: 0 1px 8px rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.7);
      transition: background 220ms ease, border-color 220ms ease;
    }

    .hero__pause:hover { background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14); border-color: #fff; }
    .hero__pause:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #fff; outline-offset: 3px; }

    .hero__pause-ico { display: grid; place-items: center; width: 26px; height: 26px; }
    .hero__pause-ico svg { width: 26px; height: 26px; }

    .hero__pause-txt {
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    /* --- 4 · The floating panel (spec §5) --------------------------------- */
    .hero__content {
      position: relative;
      z-index: 2;
      display: block;
      width: 93%;
      max-width: 1200px;
      margin-inline: auto;
      /* The decoration lives in a reserved gutter, not on top of the copy — see
         .hero__deco. Widened only where the decoration is actually shown.
         ⚠ THE padding-inline-end THAT APPLIES IT IS BELOW THE `padding`
         SHORTHAND ON PURPOSE. Declared above it, the shorthand resets it and
         the gutter silently becomes 0 — which is exactly how the decoration
         ended up back on top of the buttons once already. */
      --deco-gutter: 0px;
      /* The overhang. Spec §5. */
      margin-top: -70px;
      /* ⚠⚠ THE VERTICAL PADDING IS THE PANEL'S DEPTH, AND THE CLIENT CALLED IT
         OUT. Their comp is a shallow editorial strip; my first build ran 304px
         deep against a comp whose panel is roughly 0.18 of its own width — at
         1200px wide that is ~215px. Almost all of the excess was here (48px top
         and bottom) plus the two internal margins below.
         ⚠ RAISE THIS AND THE PANEL GETS THICK AGAIN. If the headline ever
         needs more room, take it from the type size, not from this. */
      /* ⚠ RAISED AGAIN, DELIBERATELY, AND IT IS NOT A REVERSAL. The earlier cut
         to 21px was to kill 80px of dead depth; this puts a little of it back as
         breathing room now the headline is smaller — the client's words were
         "add little space so it looks more better". Net depth is still well
         under where it started. */
      padding: clamp(20px, 2vw, 30px) clamp(28px, 3.2vw, 46px);
      padding-inline-end: calc(clamp(28px, 3.2vw, 46px) + var(--deco-gutter));
      background: #fffdf9;
      border: 1px solid rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.07);
      border-radius: 24px;
      box-shadow:
        0 1px 2px rgba(28, 14, 8, 0.04),
        0 26px 60px -26px rgba(28, 14, 8, 0.28);
    }

    /* ⚠ THE TWO COLUMNS ARE GRID PLACEMENT ON THE EXISTING CHILDREN. Middle
       track is the 1px divider (spec §6), drawn by the ::before below. */
    .hero__copy {
      display: grid;
      /* ⚠ THE RATIO IS SET BY TWO THINGS THAT BOTH HAVE TO FIT, AND THE RIGHT
         ONE NEARLY DID NOT. Left carries "Where Ballia's Future" on one line;
         right carries the two buttons on one ROW. At 1.22fr the right column
         came to 389px while the pair needs ~391px once the button padding
         reaches its cap — so above ~1900px the buttons silently wrapped and the
         panel jumped from 202px deep to 264px. 1.14fr plus a tighter gap gives
         the buttons ~415px and the headline still has room to spare.
         ⚠ IF YOU CHANGE EITHER NUMBER, check the actions row height at 1920 as
         well as the headline at 1400 — the failure is at the far end. */
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.14fr) 1px minmax(0, 1fr);
      /* Row 1 is sized by the right column's copy; row 2 by the headline. The
         left pair is centred across both by the align-self rules below. */
      grid-template-rows: auto auto;
      align-content: center;
      column-gap: clamp(24px, 2.8vw, 44px);
      row-gap: 0;
    }

    .hero__copy::before {
      content: "";
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1 / -1;
      background: rgba(18, 16, 15, 0.1);
    }

    /* ⚠ THE LEFT COLUMN IS CENTRED AS ONE BLOCK, NOT ROW BY ROW. The client's
       comp sits the eyebrow-and-headline pair optically in the middle of the
       card while the right column stays top-aligned. Centring each row
       separately would pull them apart; `align-self: end` on the eyebrow and
       `start` on the headline closes them to each other around the row
       boundary, and the row track does the centring for the pair. */
    .hero__lead {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      justify-content: center;
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 1 / -1;
    }

    .hero__eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--sb-4); }
    .hero__title   { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .hero__deck    { grid-column: 3; grid-row: 1; }
    .hero__actions { grid-column: 3; grid-row: 2; align-self: start; }

    /* ⚠ THESE TWO MOVE OR GO ON DESKTOP — THEY ARE NOT DELETED. The motto is
       rendered inside the video instead (.hero__vmotto); the stat row has no
       slot in the reference panel. Both still render on mobile, untouched. */
    .hero__motto { display: none; }
    .hero__stats { display: none; }

    /* --- 5 · Panel typography: the ground is cream now, not a photograph -- */
    .hero__eyebrow {
      color: var(--sb-text-muted);
      text-shadow: none;
    }

    /* ⚠ RULE FIRST, THEN THE WORDS. Spec §7 puts the small orange line above/
       before the eyebrow text; the markup has it after, because that is the
       order mobile draws. row-reverse flips the pair visually without touching
       the DOM, and flex-end re-packs them against the left edge (in a reversed
       row, flex-start is the RIGHT edge — dropping it pushes the eyebrow to
       the far side of the column). */
    .hero__eyebrow {
      flex-direction: row-reverse;
      justify-content: flex-end;
    }

    .hero__eyebrow-rule { background: var(--sb-violet); flex-basis: 40px; }

    .hero__title {
      color: var(--sb-ink);
      text-shadow: none;
      /* ⚠ SERIF ON THE PANEL, GROTESQUE EVERYWHERE ELSE. Spec §8 says
         "serif/display" and the client's comp draws it unmistakably as a serif
         — the headline on cream is the one place this face appears in the hero.
         Mobile still uses --sb-font-display, untouched, because this rule is
         inside min-width:1024px. */
      font-family: var(--sb-font-serif);
      /* 52 → 46 → 42. The first cut was to stop the panel being too deep; this
         one is the client asking for a calmer headline once the strip was the
         right height. Two lines at 42px still carry the card. */
      font-size: clamp(30px, 2.8vw, 42px);
      line-height: 1.02;
      letter-spacing: -0.03em;
      text-wrap: balance;
    }

    /* ⚠⚠ TWO LINES, BROKEN WHERE THE SPEC BREAKS THEM, AND DONE ENTIRELY IN
       CSS. The spec sets the headline as:
              Where Ballia's Future
              Leaders Take Shape
       The markup groups it differently — "Where Ballia's Future <Leaders>" in
       one span and "Take Shape" in another — because that is what MOBILE
       renders, and mobile must not change. So instead of re-nesting the spans
       (which would move the break on a phone too), the two spans are made
       inline here and the break is inserted before "Leaders" with a newline
       glyph. The h1 then flows as one sentence with exactly one break in it.
       ⚠ THE \A NEEDS white-space: pre TO COUNT AS A BREAK. Without it the
       content renders as a space and the headline silently becomes one line. */
    .hero__line,
    .hero__take { display: inline; }

    .hero__accent::before {
      content: "\A";
      white-space: pre;
    }

    /* ⚠ --sb-violet HERE, NOT --sb-orange-tint. The tint is the dark-ground
       orange and goes chalky on cream; the note beside .hero__accent above
       explains the pair. This is the same decision in the other direction. */
    .hero__accent { color: var(--sb-violet); }

    /* Line 2 is "Take Shape" at full headline weight in the reference — it is
       no longer the smaller third line it was when the copy sat on the film. */
    .hero__take {
      font-size: 1em;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: -0.03em;
      line-height: 1.02;
      margin-top: 0;
      /* Inline (see the rule above); the block leading it carried on the film
         would otherwise open a gap in the middle of line two. */
      display: inline;
    }

    .hero__deck {
      color: var(--sb-text-muted);
      text-shadow: none;
      /* ⚠ NO ch CAP HERE. 46ch measured narrower than the column it sits in, so
         the sentence broke to three lines and set the panel's depth. The column
         is the measure now. */
      max-width: none;
      font-size: clamp(14.5px, 1.05vw, 16px);
      line-height: 1.55;
      margin-bottom: var(--sb-5);
    }

    /* --- 6 · Buttons sit on cream, so they take the light-ground variants -- */
    .hero__actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--sb-4); }

    .hero__cta {
      min-height: 50px;
      border-radius: 999px;
      padding-inline: clamp(20px, 1.8vw, 28px);
    }

    .hero__cta.btn--on-image-primary {
      background: var(--sb-violet);
      color: #fff;
      box-shadow: 0 8px 20px -10px rgba(201, 60, 10, 0.9);
    }
    .hero__cta.btn--on-image-primary:hover { background: var(--sb-violet-dark); }

    .hero__cta.btn--on-image-secondary {
      background: #fff;
      color: var(--sb-charcoal);
      border-color: var(--sb-border-strong);
    }
    .hero__cta.btn--on-image-secondary:hover {
      border-color: var(--sb-violet);
      color: var(--sb-violet);
      background: #fff;
    }

    /* --- 6b · Panel decoration (spec §11) --------------------------------- */
    /* --- 6b · Panel decoration (spec §11, and the client's comp) ----------
       ⚠⚠ IT LIVES IN A RESERVED GUTTER, NOT ON TOP OF THE COPY. An earlier
       build floated it in the panel's bottom-right corner and it was measured
       overlapping the Admissions button at 1360, 1280, 1100 and 1024 — there is
       no size that clears at every width when it shares space with the buttons.
       Now the panel grows its own right padding (--deco-gutter) and the artwork
       sits inside that, so the copy physically cannot reach it.

       ⚠ SHOWN ONLY WHERE THE GUTTER IS AFFORDABLE. Below 1400px the panel needs
       its full width for two columns of copy, so the gutter collapses to 0 and
       the decoration is absent. It is ornament; the panel reads without it. */
    .hero__deco { display: none; }

    @media (min-width: 1400px) {
      .hero__content { --deco-gutter: 132px; }

      .hero__deco {
        display: grid;
        justify-items: center;
        align-content: center;
        gap: 6px;
        position: absolute;
        top: clamp(14px, 1.4vw, 22px);
        bottom: clamp(14px, 1.4vw, 22px);
        right: clamp(16px, 1.6vw, 26px);
        width: 116px;
        color: var(--sb-orange-tint);
        pointer-events: none;
      }
    }

    /* The comp's people glyph on the secondary button. */
    .hero__cta-people {
      display: block;
      width: 18px;
      height: 18px;
      flex: 0 0 auto;
    }

    .hero__deco-sprig {
      width: 96px;
      height: auto;
      margin-right: 10px;
      opacity: 0.55;
    }

    /* The handwritten couplet. Rotated a touch, as the comp draws it. */
    .hero__deco-script {
      display: grid;
      justify-items: start;
      font-family: 'Caveat Variable', var(--sb-font-serif), cursive;
      font-weight: 600;
      font-size: 21px;
      line-height: 1.04;
      letter-spacing: 0.01em;
      color: var(--sb-ink);
      opacity: 0.62;
      transform: rotate(-6deg);
    }

    /* Each line steps in slightly, the way a hand would write it. */
    .hero__deco-script > span:nth-child(2) { margin-left: 6px; }
    .hero__deco-script > span:nth-child(3) { margin-left: 2px; }
    .hero__deco-script > span:nth-child(4) { margin-left: 10px; }

    .hero__deco-swoosh {
      width: 78px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 2px 0 0 6px;
      color: var(--sb-orange-tint);
      opacity: 0.85;
    }

    /* --- 7 · Entrance (spec §15) ------------------------------------------
       ⚠⚠ CSS KEYFRAMES, NOT data-reveal, AND THAT IS NOT A PREFERENCE. The
       project's GSAP reveal system filters on `belowFold` — `top > innerHeight
       * 0.92` in scripts/motion-gsap.ts — so it SKIPS anything in the first
       screen. A data-reveal on this panel would never fire; the note further up
       this file calls an attribute like that "a loaded gun at the layout", and
       it is right.

       ⚠ AND CSS IS THE SAFER TOOL HERE ANYWAY. These keyframes end at their
       natural state, so if the stylesheet loads the copy is visible — there is
       no JS gate that can strand the headline at opacity 0 on the one screen
       every visitor sees.

       ⚠ THE VIDEO IS NOT ANIMATED. Spec §15: "Video appears immediately." */
    @keyframes hero-rise {
      from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(18px); }
      to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
    }

    .hero__content { animation: hero-rise 0.62s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) both; }
    .hero__title   { animation: hero-rise 0.58s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) 0.14s both; }
    .hero__deck    { animation: hero-rise 0.58s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) 0.22s both; }
    .hero__actions { animation: hero-rise 0.58s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) 0.30s both; }

    /* --- 8 · Reduced motion ---------------------------------------------- */
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      .hero__tick-rule,
      .hero__tick,
      .hero__pause { transition: none; }

      /* ⚠ `none`, AND THE ELEMENTS MUST END VISIBLE. `both` above holds the
         from-state before the delay elapses; cancelling the animation without
         this would leave the panel at opacity 0 for a reader who asked for
         less motion — the exact opposite of the accommodation. */
      .hero__content,
      .hero__title,
      .hero__deck,
      .hero__actions {
        animation: none;
        opacity: 1;
        transform: none;
      }
    }
  }

</style>

<script>
  const hero = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-hero]');

  if (hero) {
    const slides = Array.from(hero.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-slide]'));
    const caption = hero.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-hero-caption]');
    const captionText = caption?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.hero__caption-text');
    const captionTag = caption?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.hero__caption-tag');

    // A real photograph gets an editorial caption; a slot still awaiting one
    // keeps showing its shot brief under a "Needed" tag.
    const captions = slides.map((slide) => ({
      text: slide.dataset.caption ?? '',
      tag: slide.dataset.captionTag ?? '',
    }));

    const INTERVAL = 4500;
    let index = 0;
    let timer: number | undefined;

    /* ⚠ USER PAUSE IS A SEPARATE STATE FROM THE HOVER STOP, AND IT HAS TO BE.
       mouseleave calls start(); without a flag the rotation would restart the
       moment the pointer left the hero and the button would look broken. Every
       path that could resume — start(), roll(), the film `ended` handler —
       checks this first. */
    let userPaused = false;

    const pauseBtn = hero.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-hero-pause]');
    const pauseLabel = hero.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-pause-label]');
    const pauseBars = hero.querySelector<SVGGElement>('[data-pause-bars]');
    const playTri = hero.querySelector<SVGPathElement>('[data-play-tri]');
    const ticks = Array.from(hero.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-hero-tick]'));

    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
    // Auto-advance is desktop-only and motion-respecting (docs/06 §03 Mobile).
    const mayAutoplay = () =>
      !reduced.matches && window.matchMedia('(min-width: 768px)').matches;

    /* ⚠ THE FILM SLIDES RUN ON THEIR OWN CLOCK, NOT THE 4.5s INTERVAL. A drone
       clip is twenty seconds; cutting it off a fifth of the way through is worse
       than not having it. So while a video slide is playing the interval is
       stopped, and `ended` is what advances the rotation.

       ⚠ AND EVERY PATH OUT OF HERE MUST STILL ADVANCE. If the film cannot play —
       narrow viewport, reduced motion, a browser refusing muted autoplay, a 404
       on the file — the interval has to take over or the hero stops dead on
       slide one with a still. That is why the failure branch calls start(). */
    const filmOf = (i: number) => slides[i].querySelector<HTMLVideoElement>('[data-video]');

    /* ⚠ THE FILM GATE IS NOT mayAutoplay(). They were the same test once and
       that is why the films never ran on a phone: mayAutoplay() is the rule for
       the PHOTO INTERVAL, and that rule is deliberately desktop-only
       (docs/06 §03 Mobile). The client wants the drone footage on mobile too, so
       the film has its own gate — and the photo interval keeps its old
       behaviour, untouched, on both.
       ⚠ IT IS NOT "PLAY EVERYWHERE" EITHER. Data Saver and a 2G-class
       connection opt out, because a parent on a metered handset should not
       spend a megabyte on an establishing shot they did not ask for. Reduced
       motion opts out as before. In every one of those cases the poster still
       carries the slide. */
    const link = (navigator as any).connection;
    const thrifty = () =>
      link?.saveData === true || /(^|-)2g$/.test(link?.effectiveType ?? '');
    const mayPlayFilm = () => !reduced.matches && !thrifty();
    const narrow = () => !window.matchMedia('(min-width: 768px)').matches;

    const attach = (video: HTMLVideoElement) => {
      if (video.src) return;
      const src = narrow() ? video.dataset.srcSm ?? video.dataset.src : video.dataset.src;
      if (src) video.src = src;
    };

    const rest = (i: number) => {
      const film = filmOf(i);
      if (!film) return;
      film.removeAttribute('data-playing');
      film.pause();
      /* ⚠ NO currentTime = 0 HERE. Seeking a finished clip back to the start
         makes the browser re-request the file — a second download of the whole
         video, which showed up as hero-1-sm.mp4 being fetched twice in one
         pass. roll() already rewinds immediately before it plays, which is the
         only moment the position actually matters. */
    };

    const roll = (i: number) => {
      const film = filmOf(i);
      if (!film || !mayPlayFilm() || userPaused) return false;
      attach(film);
      stop();
      film.currentTime = 0;
      film
        .play()
        .then(() => film.setAttribute('data-playing', ''))
        .catch(() => {
          /* Autoplay refused. Leave the poster showing and hand the slide back
             to the timer rather than stranding the rotation here. */
          film.removeAttribute('data-playing');
          start();
        });
      return true;
    };

    const show = (next: number) => {
      if (next === index) return;
      slides[index].removeAttribute('data-active');
      slides[index].setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      rest(index);

      index = (next + slides.length) % slides.length;

      slides[index].setAttribute('data-active', '');
      slides[index].setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'false');
      if (!roll(index)) start();

      /* The ticks are the only visible statement of where the rotation is, so
         they move with it — including when a swipe or a film `ended` drives it,
         not just when the button is clicked. */
      ticks.forEach((t, i) =>
        t.setAttribute('aria-current', i === index ? 'true' : 'false'),
      );

      // The caption belongs to the image, so it cross-fades with it.
      if (caption && captionText && captionTag) {
        caption.classList.add('is-fading');
        window.setTimeout(() => {
          captionText.textContent = captions[index].text;
          captionTag.textContent = captions[index].tag;
          caption.classList.remove('is-fading');
        }, 450);
      }
    };

    const stop = () => {
      if (timer) window.clearInterval(timer);
      timer = undefined;
    };

    const start = () => {
      stop();
      if (userPaused) return;
      if (!mayAutoplay()) return;
      /* ⚠ A PLAYING FILM OWNS THE CLOCK — DO NOT ARM THE INTERVAL OVER IT. Every
         hover out of the hero calls start(), so without this guard leaving the
         mouse would set a 4.5s timer running underneath a twenty-second clip and
         chop it off four seconds in. `ended` is what advances a film slide. */
      if (filmOf(index)?.hasAttribute('data-playing')) return;
      timer = window.setInterval(() => show(index + 1), INTERVAL);
    };

    /* ⚠ THE COMMENT THAT USED TO SIT HERE SAID THE PAUSE BUTTON HAD BEEN
       REMOVED AND HOVER WAS THE ONLY WAY TO STOP THE ROTATION. That is no
       longer true: the client asked for the control back on 17 Sep 2026 and it
       is the `.hero__pause` button in the in-video UI. Hover and focus still
       stop the timer as a convenience; the button is the explicit control, and
       it is the one that survives the pointer leaving. */
    hero.addEventListener('mouseenter', stop);
    hero.addEventListener('mouseleave', start);
    hero.addEventListener('focusin', stop);
    hero.addEventListener('focusout', start);

    /* ── The pause / play control ───────────────────────────────────────────
       ⚠ IT PAUSES BOTH CLOCKS. A photo slide is held by `timer`; a film slide
       is held by the video element itself and ignores the interval entirely
       (see the note on roll()). Stopping only one of them looks like a button
       that works on some slides and not others. */
    const paintPause = () => {
      if (!pauseBtn) return;
      pauseBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', userPaused ? 'true' : 'false');
      if (pauseLabel) pauseLabel.textContent = userPaused ? 'Play Video' : 'Pause Video';
      if (pauseBars) pauseBars.toggleAttribute('hidden', userPaused);
      if (playTri) playTri.toggleAttribute('hidden', !userPaused);
    };

    const setPaused = (next: boolean) => {
      userPaused = next;
      const film = filmOf(index);
      if (next) {
        stop();
        film?.pause();
      } else if (film?.hasAttribute('data-playing')) {
        /* Mid-clip: hand it straight back to the video, not to the interval —
           arming a 4.5s timer over a twenty-second film is the bug the note on
           start() warns about. */
        film.play().catch(() => start());
      } else {
        start();
      }
      paintPause();
    };

    pauseBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => setPaused(!userPaused));

    /* ⚠ A TICK CLICK CLEARS THE PAUSE. Asking for a specific slide is asking to
       see it move on; leaving the hero frozen after an explicit choice reads as
       a broken control. */
    ticks.forEach((tick, i) =>
      tick.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if (userPaused) setPaused(false);
        show(i);
      }),
    );

    paintPause();

    // Swipe — the only way to advance on mobile.
    let touchX = 0;
    hero.addEventListener(
      'touchstart',
      (event) => { touchX = event.changedTouches[0].clientX; },
      { passive: true }
    );
    hero.addEventListener(
      'touchend',
      (event) => {
        const delta = event.changedTouches[0].clientX - touchX;
        if (Math.abs(delta) > 48) show(index + (delta < 0 ? 1 : -1));
      },
      { passive: true }
    );

    slides.forEach((_, i) => {
      const film = filmOf(i);
      film?.addEventListener('ended', () => {
        film.removeAttribute('data-playing');
        show(index + 1);
      });
      /* Same failure mode by another route: a clip that 404s or fails to decode
         never reaches `ended`. Hand the slide back to the interval rather than
         leaving the hero parked on a still forever. */
      film?.addEventListener('error', () => {
        film.removeAttribute('data-playing');
        start();
      });
    });

    /* ⚠⚠ DO NOT PAUSE THE FILM ON HOVER. IT DEADLOCKS THE WHOLE ROTATION.
       An earlier version did exactly that, to match the timer's pause-on-hover.
       But a cursor resting anywhere over the hero — which is where it sits while
       anyone is actually looking at the page — paused the clip about a second
       in, and a paused clip never fires `ended`, and `ended` is the only thing
       that advances a film slide. The hero stopped dead on slide one and no
       photograph was ever reached. It reproduces instantly: load the page and
       leave the mouse over the banner.

       So hover governs the INTERVAL only, which is what it was written for. The
       film plays through and hands off when it ends, hovered or not. */

    reduced.addEventListener('change', start);

    /* Slide one is a film, so the opening move is to roll it; start() is the
       fallback for every case where it will not run. */
    if (!roll(index)) start();
  }
</script>
